---
title: Defining a query handler
description: IQueryHandler and IStreamQueryHandler — returning a value, or streaming one row at a time.
---

A query handler implements `IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>`:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

As everywhere else: `ValueTask` return, cancellation on the context, dependencies through the
constructor, and no registration call — the source generator finds it.

## One handler per query

Like commands, a query has exactly one main handler. Two registrations for the same query
type raise `MultipleHandlerFoundException` at dispatch.

## Streaming

`IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<TResult>` instead:

Note the method name: streaming handlers implement **`StreamAsync`**, not `HandleAsync`, and
there is no separate cancellation-token parameter.

```csharp
public sealed class StreamProductsHandler : IStreamQueryHandler<StreamProducts, Product>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public StreamProductsHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public async IAsyncEnumerable<Product> StreamAsync(
        StreamProducts query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        // Read what you need off the context before the first yield: from here on the
        // method body runs during the consumer's enumeration, not during the dispatch call.
        var cancellationToken = context.CancellationToken;

        await foreach (var page in _store.PagesAsync(query.PageSize, cancellationToken))
            foreach (var product in page)
                yield return product;
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
An iterator method body does not start running until the consumer enumerates it. Capture
anything you need from `ErgosfareContext` into locals before the first `yield return` rather
than touching `context` inside the loop — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).
{% /aside %}

Consume it with `await foreach`:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in _queries.StreamAsync(new StreamProducts(100), ct))
    await WriteAsync(product);
```

## Returning "not found"

If the query is declared `IQuery<Product?>`, returning `null` is the contract and callers must
handle it. If it is declared `IQuery<Product>`, throw — and let an
[exception interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors)
translate that into whatever the edge of your application returns:

```csharp
public ValueTask<Product> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken)
        ?? throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Product {query.Id} not found");
```

Pick one and be consistent across the codebase; mixing the two is what makes callers write
both a null check and a try/catch.

## Caching

The pipeline is a better place for caching than the handler, because it keeps the handler
about the data:

- The handler checks the cache first and returns the hit — nothing before the handler can
  supply the pipeline's result, so the read side belongs here or in a handler that decorates
  the real one.
- A [post-interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors) populates
  the cache on the way out.

Scope both to a marker interface such as `ICacheableQuery` so only the queries you opted in
pay for it.

## Lifetimes

Handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on every dispatch. A query handler that holds a
`DbContext` should be **scoped**, matching the context's own lifetime — a singleton handler
capturing a scoped dependency is the usual source of "cannot access a disposed object" at
runtime. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

